"{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1126/science.1170391\",\n  \"title\": \"Entropic Evidence for Linguistic Structure in the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Rajesh P. N. Rao; Nisha Yadav; Mayank N. Vahia; Hrishikesh Joglekar; R. Adhikari; Iravatham Mahadevan\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>Analysis of the pattern of symbols confirms the linguistic role of ancient signs.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1126/science.1170391\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2009-05-29T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1170391\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 47,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'American Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)', 'container_title': 'Science', 'volume': '324', 'issue': '5931', 'page': '1165-1165', 'issn': ['0036-8075', '1095-9203'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '221', 'prefix': '10.1126'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1038/nindia.2009.128\",\n  \"title\": \"Indus valley script is linguistic\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1038/nindia.2009.128\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2009-05-08T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nindia.2009.128\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Springer Science and Business Media LLC', 'container_title': 'Nature India', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': ['1755-3180'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '297', 'prefix': '10.1038'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2139/ssrn.7436801\",\n  \"title\": \"Sequential Structure Without a Linguistic Diagnosis: A Falsification-Oriented Analysis of the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Joy Bose\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>The Indus Valley script remains undeciphered. Under ordinary cross-validated scoring, real Indus sign sequences show a positive trigram-over-bigram predictive gain (the two preceding signs predict the next sign better than one preceding sign alone), replicating closely across two independently extracted digitizations of the same underlying corpus tradition, including the M77 corpus corresponding to the concordance behind the most cited statistical studies of the script (+0.143 and +0.158 bits), and clearing 200-trial null distributions entirely. However, both corpora contain substantial exact-sequence repetition (23 to 33 percent of sequences). When cross-validation folds are constructed so duplicate sequences cannot appear in both training and their own held-out evaluation, the gain reverses sign for both corpora (-0.088,-0.083); when duplicates are instead collapsed to unique sequence types, it weakens to near zero (-0.023,-0.010). A control experiment, grouping sequences of the same size distribution but without duplicate identity, stays strongly positive, ruling out the alternative explanation that this reversal simply reflects smaller effective training sets rather than duplicate identity specifically. This is not a data-handling error, since the repetition is a genuine corpus property, but it means the ordinary-CV result should not be read as established evidence of sequential structure that generalizes to novel sequences, and we treat resolving this as the paper's central open problem. Independently of that question, our most specific test among the mechanisms we examined shows the ordinary-CV gain is not uniquely diagnostic of the particular linguistic generative mechanism we tested, including after rebuilding that generator and a non-linguistic alternative to match the real corpus's own vocabulary and length statistics; neither reproduces the real magnitude in matched or unmatched form. A sample-size curve on two calibration languages shows this estimator rarely returns a positive result below roughly 3,000 sequences for either, evidence against the ordinary-CV result being generic small-sample behavior. We also find evidence against inscriptions being deliberately constructed as globally unique identifiers, though not against more elaborate identifier schemes. The framework, derived corpora where licensing permits, and all experiments are released openly.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2139/ssrn.7436801\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7436801\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Elsevier BV', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '78', 'prefix': '10.2139'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.31235/osf.io/3amvt_v1\",\n  \"title\": \"Sequential Structure Without a Linguistic Diagnosis: A Falsification-Oriented Analysis of the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Joy Bose\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<p>The Indus Valley script remains undeciphered. Under ordinary cross-validated scoring, real Indus sign sequences show a positive trigram-over-bigram predictive gain (the two preceding signs predict the next sign better than one preceding sign alone), replicating closely across two independently extracted digitizations of the same underlying corpus tradition, including the M77 corpus corresponding to the concordance behind the most cited statistical studies of the script (+0.143 and +0.158 bits), and clearing 200-trial null distributions entirely. However, both corpora contain substantial exact-sequence repetition (23 to 33 percent of sequences). When cross-validation folds are constructed so duplicate sequences cannot appear in both training and their own held-out evaluation, the gain reverses sign for both corpora (-0.088, -0.083); when duplicates are instead collapsed to unique sequence types, it weakens to near zero (-0.023, -0.010). A control experiment, grouping sequences of the same size distribution but without duplicate identity, stays strongly positive, ruling out the alternative explanation that this reversal simply reflects smaller effective training sets rather than duplicate identity specifically. This is not a data-handling error, since the repetition is a genuine corpus property, but it means the ordinary-CV result should not be read as established evidence of sequential structure that generalizes to novel sequences, and we treat resolving this as the paper's central open problem. Independently of that question, our most specific test among the mechanisms we examined shows the ordinary-CV gain is not uniquely diagnostic of the particular linguistic generative mechanism we tested, including after rebuilding that generator and a non-linguistic alternative to match the real corpus's own vocabulary and length statistics; neither reproduces the real magnitude in matched or unmatched form. A sample-size curve on two calibration languages shows this estimator rarely returns a positive result below roughly 3,000 sequences for either, evidence against the ordinary-CV result being generic small-sample behavior. We also find evidence against inscriptions being deliberately constructed as globally unique identifiers, though not against more elaborate identifier schemes. The framework, derived corpora where licensing permits, and all experiments are released openly.</p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.31235/osf.io/3amvt_v1\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2026-09-09T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/3amvt_v1\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Center for Open Science', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '15934', 'prefix': '10.31235'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.70193/ijlsh.v2i2.238\",\n  \"title\": \"Deciphering the Indus Valley/Harappan Script: A Comparative Linguistic Approach\",\n  \"authors\": \"Rishabh Bhargava\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>Symbols were taken from intact seals of the Indus Valley/Harappan civilisation and analysed for similarity to other known languages. Identical symbols were demonstrated. Text from 8 Indus Script seals were analysed and appeared to be related to the Germanic languages. The symbols identical to the Germanic languages were present in multiple seals from the Indus Valley/Harappan period and, therefore, may help elucidate some of the meaning of the Indus Script.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.70193/ijlsh.v2i2.238\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2025-07-09T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"https://journal.lps2h.com/ijlsh/article/download/238/172\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.70193/ijlsh.v2i2.238\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Sekolah Tinggi Ilmu Hukum Al-Banna', 'container_title': 'International Journal of Law, Social Science, and Humanities', 'volume': '2', 'issue': '2', 'page': '232-244', 'issn': ['3063-7554'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '50908', 'prefix': '10.70193'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1162/coli_c_00030\",\n  \"title\": \"Entropy, the Indus Script, and Language:A Reply to R. Sproat\",\n  \"authors\": \"Rajesh P. N. Rao; Nisha Yadav; Mayank N. Vahia; Hrishikesh Joglekar; Ronojoy Adhikari; Iravatham Mahadevan\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1162/coli_c_00030\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2010-12-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1162/coli_c_00030\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 16,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'MIT Press - Journals', 'container_title': 'Computational Linguistics', 'volume': '36', 'issue': '4', 'page': '795-805', 'issn': ['0891-2017', '1530-9312'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '281', 'prefix': '10.1162'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.21275/sr251216111751\",\n  \"title\": \"Sanskrit Influence on the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Jeyakumar Ramasami\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.21275/sr251216111751\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2025-12-20T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.21275/sr251216111751\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'International Journal of Science and Research', 'container_title': 'International Journal of Science and Research (IJSR)', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '1329-1334', 'issn': ['2319-7064'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '8810', 'prefix': '10.21275'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2139/ssrn.7109939\",\n  \"title\": \"A Comparative Linguistic Model of the Indus Script Based on Proto-Indo-Aryan Geochronology and Archaic Pre-Vedic Orthography\",\n  \"authors\": \"Sagar More\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>This paper presents a comparative linguistic model evaluating the structural and phonetic relationships between the Indus script corpus and early Vedic orthography. Grounded in recent palaeo-hydrological timelines of the Saraswati River basin and late Bronze Age geochronology, the study establishes a contextual framework positioning the Indus Valley Civilization within the formative periods of Proto-Indo-Aryan development. Moving away from arbitrary signmatching, this research introduces a systematic cryptographic decoding methodology based on early Sanskrit grammatical paradigms and morphosyntactic structures. By applying these specific phonological constraints to the standard Indus seal sign distributions, the model evaluates whether the script operates as a logosyllabic system with a core consonantal framework. Preliminary results suggest a high degree of structural consistency when testing Indus graphemic sequences against Vedic Sanskrit phonetic rules, providing a reproducible pathway for analyzing sign-to-sound correspondences in the Indus corpus.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2139/ssrn.7109939\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7109939\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Elsevier BV', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '78', 'prefix': '10.2139'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1038/nindia.2009.147\",\n  \"title\": \"Write signs for Indus script?\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1038/nindia.2009.147\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2009-05-31T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nindia.2009.147\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Springer Science and Business Media LLC', 'container_title': 'Nature India', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': ['1755-3180'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '297', 'prefix': '10.1038'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.31219/osf.io/aqey4\",\n  \"title\": \"Indus script Decipherment\",\n  \"authors\": \"Ramkumar Unnipillai Santhakumari\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<p>A  partial decipherment of the  Indus script is described here. Similarities between Protocuneiform characters and Indus script characters were observed. Transferring meanings  from Protocuneiform to Indus script characters using ProtoDravidian sounds  resulted in meaningful readings .  These meanings as well as relations observed between Indus script and Brahmi script are described here.</p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.31219/osf.io/aqey4\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2019-10-02T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.31219/osf.io/aqey4\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Center for Open Science', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '15934', 'prefix': '10.31219'}\"\n}"
